# {{name}}

Generated by `pnpm turbo gen remote` in **standalone mode** — an
independent project, outside the `enterprise-microfrontend-boilerplate`
monorepo, that consumes `@enterprise-mfe/*` as ordinary published
dependencies rather than `workspace:*` links (ADR-0007).

## Install prerequisite — read this before `pnpm install`

This project's `package.json` depends on `@enterprise-mfe/auth`,
`@enterprise-mfe/event-bus`, `@enterprise-mfe/shared-types`, and
`@enterprise-mfe/ui` as published packages resolved from GitHub Packages
(`.npmrc`, `@enterprise-mfe` scope). **`pnpm install` will fail until those
packages have been published to GitHub Packages at least once.** If no
maintainer of the source monorepo has run its publish workflow yet, this
failure is expected, not a bug in this generated project — see
`docs/decisions/0014-generator-dual-mode.md` and
`.github/workflows/publish-packages.yml` in the source monorepo.

You will also need `GITHUB_TOKEN` (a token with `read:packages` scope for
that project's GitHub org/user) exported in your shell before installing —
`.npmrc` reads it from the environment, it is never written to a file here.

## Running it

Standalone, with no shell present — a remote is a portable application in
its own right:

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm dev      # http://localhost:{{port}}
pnpm build
```

## Composing it inside a shell

This project's own generator has no registry it owns to write to (FR-020) —
register this remote in a consuming shell's own remote registry by adding
an entry shaped like:

```jsonc
{
  "name": "{{name}}",
  "entry": "https://<where-this-remote-is-deployed>/mf-manifest.json",
  "routePath": "{{routePath}}",
  "label": "{{title}}"
}
```

## Structure

The same `src/exposed/` / `src/internal/` split every remote in the source
monorepo uses:

```text
src/
├── exposed/
│   └── App.tsx              # what a shell mounts ("{{name}}/App")
├── internal/
│   └── placeholder/          # replace with this remote's real feature
├── bootstrap.tsx             # standalone dev entry
└── index.tsx                 # dynamic import('./bootstrap') — the MF async boundary
```

## What's deliberately not here

- No real feature — this is placeholder content, structurally correct and
  ready to build on (generator spec Assumptions).
- No CI/CD — set that up for wherever this project actually gets deployed.
- No production remote registration — that is a deployment decision for
  whichever shell composes this remote, not something a scaffolding tool
  should do automatically.
